{
  "_": "THE SCALAR FLOOR AND THE FRESHNESS CORPUS. `cases` carries ONE ENTRY PER DECLARED SCALAR and is the coverage floor `tools/ast_conformance.py --coverage` enforces; `trees` carries hand-derived formulas whose freshness verdict is asserted in BOTH LANES against the `freshness` field below.",
  "_why_its_own_file": "\u26d4 A SCALAR HAS NO BAR BEHAVIOUR, SO IT DOES NOT BELONG IN THE BAR CORPUS. `assert_corpus_covers_the_table` demands a corpus case per declared name and ABORTS the recorder otherwise; folding 54 scalars into that floor would force 54 new bar-corpus cases, move every one of the 17 frozen per-ast digests, and re-freeze a cross-lane oracle for a reason that has nothing to do with bars. Two floors, two fixtures, two things measured.",
  "_shape": "`cases[]` is `{id, scalar, ast, value, asOf, cadence, yields}` -- `ast` is the CANONICAL tree (a bare `series` node, because a scalar rides that node type and the canonical vocabulary stays four). `value` is a distinct sample so a mis-seeded name is visible rather than plausible. `trees[]` is `{id, ast, freshness, repaint, scalars, why}` and every entry states its own reason.",
  "_both_verdicts": "\u2b50 EVERY `trees` ENTRY DECLARES BOTH `repaint` AND `freshness`, ON THE SAME TREE, ON PURPOSE. The repaint linter answers a true 0 for a scalar and brands it `non-repainting`; that is correct and it is why a second verdict exists. Asserting them together is what makes the pair impossible to obtain one at a time.",
  "bars": 6,
